{"name":"White Mink","hex":"#efeee9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"mink","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any semi-aquatic, carnivorous mammal in the Mustelinae subfamily, similar to weasels, with dark fur, native to Europe and America, of which two species in different genera are extant: the American mink (Neogale vison) and the European mink (Mustela lutreola).","The fur or pelt of a mink, used to make apparel."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mink"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mink","blocks":[{"text":"Mink are dark-coloured, semiaquatic, carnivorous mammals in the genera Mustela and Neogale and part of the family Mustelidae, which also includes weasels, stoats, martens, otters, badgers, and others. There are two extant species referred to as \"mink\", the American mink and the European mink. The extinct sea mink was related to the American mink but was much larger."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/American_Mink.jpg/330px-American_Mink.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mink"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Europ%C3%A4ischer_Nerz.jpg/500px-Europ%C3%A4ischer_Nerz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Europ%C3%A4ischer_Nerz.jpg/500px-Europ%C3%A4ischer_Nerz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"European mink (Mustela lutreola)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEurop%C3%A4ischer_Nerz.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Mink_with_crayfish_at_Wascana_Lake_in_Regina_Saskatchewan.jpg/500px-Mink_with_crayfish_at_Wascana_Lake_in_Regina_Saskatchewan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Mink_with_crayfish_at_Wascana_Lake_in_Regina_Saskatchewan.jpg/1280px-Mink_with_crayfish_at_Wascana_Lake_in_Regina_Saskatchewan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Mink_with_crayfish_at_Wascana_Lake_in_Regina_Saskatchewan.jpg/500px-Mink_with_crayfish_at_Wascana_Lake_in_Regina_Saskatchewan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mink eating a crayfish","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMink_with_crayfish_at_Wascana_Lake_in_Regina_Saskatchewan.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Mink_in_the_snow.png/500px-Mink_in_the_snow.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src"